\description{
Estimates abundance, recruitment, and mortality from catch-at-age and survey
observations. Returns population trends, observation diagnostics, parameter
estimates, and uncertainty tables. Use \code{N_settings}, \code{F_settings}, and
\code{M_settings} to specify where unexplained biological variation is allowed.
See \code{\link[=prepare_tam]{prepare_tam()}} for options and \link{tinyAM-model} for the model equations.
}
\details{
Builds data with \code{\link[=prepare_tam]{prepare_tam()}} and initial parameters with \code{\link[=make_par]{make_par()}}.
RTMB integrates random effects using the Laplace approximation; \code{nlminb}
optimizes the remaining fixed effects. \code{\link[=mono]{mono()}} increments \code{dq} and \code{dmu_f} have a zero
lower bound, as do initial-abundance decrements \code{d_n0}. Their SEs remain on the increment scale, but symmetric Wald
intervals are only local approximations at a boundary.
Random-effect blocks are chosen from the active processes and Gaussian
formula effects. Initial abundance and recruitment boundaries are integrated
only when their respective \code{init} setting is \code{"latent"}; see \link{tinyAM-model}
and \link{recruitment_formulas}. \code{\link[=make_par]{make_par()}} describes the parameter layout.

A warning is issued if the number of random effects exceeds 1.5 times the
number of observed data points (rough identifiability check).

Parameters without likelihood support are removed or fixed: examples include
constant mean directions canceled by RW increments, RW SDs with no increments,
and AR1 correlations on singleton axes. The latent states themselves retain
the conventions in \link{tinyAM-model}.

\code{is_converged} summarizes optimizer, gradient and uncertainty checks. Also inspect
\code{opt$convergence} and \code{opt$message}; a passing numerical check does not
establish that the data identify every biological component.
Fitting also warns about sparse catchability effects and wide formula-effect
uncertainty. These advisories are retained by \code{\link[=check_tam]{check_tam()}} and do not change
the likelihood, parameter constraints or numerical convergence criteria.

Omitted entries in a settings list use package defaults; \code{\link[=update]{update()}} instead
retains the original fit's entries. Use explicit \code{NULL} to remove an optional
formula or supplied offset. Unknown or duplicate entry names are errors.
Supply spawning timing in \code{data$maturity$spawn_time}; see \code{\link[=prepare_tam]{prepare_tam()}}.
}
\examples{
fit <- fit_tam(
  cod_obs,
  years = 1983:2024,
  ages = 2:14,
  N_settings = list(process = "iid", init = "exp"),
  F_settings = list(process = "rw", mu_form = NULL),
  M_settings = list(process = "off", mu_supplied = ~ I(0.3)),
  catch_settings = list(sd_form = ~ 1),
  index_settings = list(sd_form = ~ 1, q_form = ~ q_block),
  silent = TRUE
)
fit
head(fit$pop$ssb)

## Fit with projections (status quo F)
fit2 <- update(fit,
  proj_settings = list(n_proj = 3, n_mean = 3, F_mult = 1)
)

if (interactive()) {
  fits <- list("without_proj" = fit, "with_proj" = fit2)
  vis_tam(fits)
}

}
